      why: 'A thread is read in glances, not in full. The mark is what the eye resolves before any label does, so it is doing the identification work whether it was designed for it or not. A generic circle, a stock headshot or a borrowed product logo all quietly claim something untrue.',
      when: 'Anywhere the agent authors, acts or waits: every generated turn, every activity entry, every surface where its work sits beside a person’s.',
      how: 'Draw it from a container role and put the reserved glyph inside it, never a face and never initials — initials are what people get. Keep one silhouette across every size and let only the glyph scale. Let the mark carry working state as a ring, and drop the ring the moment work stops.',
      expressive: 'This is the neural expressive idea at its smallest: the mark is primary-container with the reserved glyph in primary, while a person is surface-container-highest with their initials — a different role, not a different shade, so the two separate before either is read. Working state is a ring animating on the mark itself rather than a spinner somewhere else on the screen, because state belongs to whatever it is a state of. Shape is corner-full throughout; only the glyph scales between 28, 40 and 56.',
